          "quote": "Let $\\mathcal X$ be a finite nonempty candidate set, let $p\\ge1$, and let $f:\\mathcal X\\to\\mathbb R^p$ be the declared regression-vector map. Work under the stated linear mean model $Y=f(x)^{\\mathsf T}\\beta+\\epsilon$, with mean-zero independent run errors of common variance $\\sigma^2>0$. These are modeling assumptions, not claims about an experiment's empirical adequacy.\n\nAn **approximate design** is a weight function $\\xi:\\mathcal X\\to[0,1]$ with $\\sum_{x\\in\\mathcal X}\\xi_x=1$. Its support is $\\operatorname{supp}(\\xi)=\\{x:\\xi_x>0\\}$ and its information matrix is\n$$M(\\xi)=\\sum_{x\\in\\mathcal X}\\xi_x f(x)f(x)^{\\mathsf T}.$$\nThis matrix is symmetric and positive semidefinite. A design is **nonsingular** when $M(\\xi)$ is positive definite. For a singular design, the page's estimability convention is that a specified coefficient contrast $c^{\\mathsf T}\\beta$ is estimable exactly when $c\\in\\operatorname{span}\\{f(x):x\\in\\operatorname{supp}(\\xi)\\}$; the inverse-matrix criteria below are defined only for nonsingular designs.\n\nFor nonsingular designs on this fixed $f$ and $\\mathcal X$, **D-optimality** maximizes $\\log\\det M(\\xi)$, **A-optimality** minimizes $\\operatorname{tr}(M(\\xi)^{-1})$, **E-optimality** maximizes $\\lambda_{\\min}(M(\\xi))$, and **$c$-optimality** for a fixed $c\\in\\mathbb R^p$ minimizes $c^{\\mathsf T}M(\\xi)^{-1}c$; $c=0$ gives a trivial tie. The smallest eigenvalue is real because $M(\\xi)$ is real symmetric ([[cor-real-spectral-theorem-for-self-adjoint-endomorphisms]]). These are different model- and candidate-dependent objectives; none is a universal design ranking.\n\nAn **exact $n$-run design**, for an integer $n\\ge1$, has weights $\\xi_x=n_x/n$ with nonnegative integer counts $n_x$ summing to $n$; optimizing over those integer allocations is a separate finite problem.\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "alg-finite-budget-multiplicative-d-optimal-design",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $p\\ge1$, let $\\mathcal X$ be a finite nonempty candidate set, and let $f_x\\in\\mathbb R^p$ be the feature vector for each $x\\in\\mathcal X$. An approximate design $\\xi$ and information matrix $M(\\xi)=\\sum_x\\xi_xf_xf_x^T$ use the convention of [[def-approximate-linear-model-design-and-optimality-criteria]]. The same vectors may be scalar-observation sensitivities at a fixed parameter, as in [[def-nonlinear-experimental-design-and-local-identifiability]], with a scalar precision absorbed into $f_x$. The procedure uses exact arithmetic.\n\n**Input:** A finite nonempty $\\mathcal X$, $p\\ge1$, its feature vectors $(f_x)_{x\\in\\mathcal X}$, an approximate initial design $\\xi^{(0)}$, and a finite nonnegative integer budget $N$ counting weight updates.\n\n**Procedure:** Form $M_0=M(\\xi^{(0)})$. If $M_0$ is not positive definite, return $(\\xi^{(0)},\\text{undefined violation},\\text{invalid initial design})$. Otherwise, for $k=0,1,\\ldots,N$, with current $\\xi^{(k)}$ and $M_k=M(\\xi^{(k)})$, compute\n$$s_x^{(k)}=f_x^TM_k^{-1}f_x\\quad(x\\in\\mathcal X),\\qquad v_k=\\max_{x\\in\\mathcal X}s_x^{(k)}-p.$$\nIf $v_k=0$, return $(\\xi^{(k)},v_k,\\text{equivalence-certified})$. If $k=N$, return $(\\xi^{(k)},v_k,\\text{budget-exhausted})$. Otherwise set\n$$\\xi_x^{(k+1)}=\\xi_x^{(k)}\\frac{s_x^{(k)}}p\\quad(x\\in\\mathcal X),$$\nform $M_{k+1}=\\sum_x\\xi_x^{(k+1)}f_xf_x^T$, and continue. The proved update invariant keeps every later $M_k$ positive definite.\n\nThe budget guarantees at most $N$ updates and $N+1$ sensitivity scans. A nonzero returned violation is reported as-is; it is not an exact D-optimality certificate. The exact integer allocation problem remains separate from this approximate-weight procedure.\n",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-multiplicative-design-update-well-defined-and-terminates",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For the algorithm [[alg-finite-budget-multiplicative-d-optimal-design]], suppose the initial weights form an approximate design and its information matrix is positive definite. Then every update preserves nonnegative weights summing to one and positive definiteness of the information matrix. For every finite budget $N$, the procedure returns after at most $N$ updates and at most $N+1$ sensitivity scans.\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-equivalence-violation-bounds-d-optimal-design-gap",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $\\mathcal X$ be a finite nonempty candidate set, let $p\\ge1$, and let $\\xi$ be any returned nonsingular approximate design from [[alg-finite-budget-multiplicative-d-optimal-design]]. Put $M=M(\\xi)$ and\n$$s_x=f_x^TM^{-1}f_x,\\qquad v=\\max_{x\\in\\mathcal X}s_x-p.$$\nThen $v\\ge0$, every nonsingular approximate competitor $\\eta$ satisfies\n$$\\log\\det M(\\eta)-\\log\\det M(\\xi)\\le v,$$\nand $v=0$ if and only if $\\xi$ is D-optimal among nonsingular approximate designs.\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-logarithm-derivative-and-integral",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For $x>0$, $\\log$ is differentiable and\n$$\\log'(x)=\\frac1x,\\qquad \\log x=\\int_1^x\\frac{dt}{t}.$$\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "cor-mean-value-theorem",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $a, b \\in \\mathbb{R}$ with $a < b$ and let $f : [a,b] \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be\ncontinuous on $[a,b]$ ([[def-continuity-real]], [[def-interval]]) and\ndifferentiable at every point of $(a,b)$ as a function on $[a,b]$\n([[def-derivative]]). Then there is $c \\in (a,b)$ with\n\n$$f(b) - f(a) \\;=\\; f'(c)\\,(b - a) .$$\n\nEquivalently, since $b - a \\ne 0$, there is $c \\in (a,b)$ at which\n$f'(c) = \\bigl(f(b)-f(a)\\bigr)/(b-a)$: the derivative somewhere inside equals\nthe average rate of change across the whole interval.\n\n**Continuity on the closed interval cannot be dropped.** Differentiability at\nevery point of $(a,b)$ alone does not suffice: a function on $[0,1]$,\ndifferentiable at every point of $(0,1)$ with derivative constantly $1$, for\nwhich no $c$ works, is exhibited later on this page as a false statement, and\nthe companion page works the same witness out in full.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "source_guards": {
    "items/ex-endpoint-sensors-give-a-d-optimal-linear-time-design.md": "---\nid: ex-endpoint-sensors-give-a-d-optimal-linear-time-design\nkind: example\ntitle: \"Endpoint sensor times are D-optimal for an intercept-slope model\"\nstatus: draft\norigin: pipeline\npipeline_run: numerical-optimization-dynamics-2026-10-16\ndependency_level: 4\ndeps:\n  - alg-finite-budget-multiplicative-d-optimal-design\n  - def-approximate-linear-model-design-and-optimality-criteria\n  - def-nonlinear-experimental-design-and-local-identifiability\n  - thm-equivalence-violation-bounds-d-optimal-design-gap\n  - thm-multiplicative-design-update-well-defined-and-terminates\n  - thm-logarithm-derivative-and-integral\n  - cor-mean-value-theorem\njustified_by: []\nproof_strategy: direct\nprovenance:\n  statement: ai-generated\n  proof: ai-generated\ngeneration:\n  role: example\nverification:\n  precheck: pass\nsources:\n  references:\n    - title: \"Stephen Boyd and Lieven Vandenberghe, Convex Optimization, §7.5\"\n      url: \"https://web.stanford.edu/~boyd/cvxbook/bv_cvxbook.pdf\"\n      locator: \"The complete book and §7.5 were read for approximate designs, D-optimality, and the separate integer allocation problem.\"\n    - title: \"Holger Dette, Andrey Pepelyshev, and Anatoly Zhigljavsky, Improving Updating Rules in Multiplicative Algorithms for Computing D-optimal Designs, §2\"\n      url: \"https://eldorado.tu-dortmund.de/bitstreams/45748862-e3e2-4e73-b8a5-7528e8b409c0/download\"\n      locator: \"The complete 15-page paper and §2 were read for the multiplicative update recurrence and sensitivity function.\"\n---\n\n## Example\n\nFor the linear time-response model $F_t(\\theta)=\\theta_0+\\theta_1t$ with candidate sensor times $t\\in\\{0,1,2\\}$ and unit precision, the equal-weight endpoint design is D-optimal. Starting from uniform weights and using a one-update budget, the multiplicative procedure returns a positive equivalence violation instead of claiming exact optimality.\n\n## Facts & Assumptions\n\n**Given:** Candidate features $f_t=(1,t)^T$ at $t=0,1,2$, parameter dimension $p=2$, unit precision, and initial approximate design $\\xi^{(0)}=(1/3,1/3,1/3)$ in that order.\n\n[F1] [[def-approximate-linear-model-design-and-optimality-criteria]]: $M(\\xi)=\\sum_t\\xi_tf_tf_t^T$ and D-optimality maximizes $\\log\\det M(\\xi)$ among nonsingular approximate designs.\n\n[F2] [[alg-finite-budget-multiplicative-d-optimal-design]]: with $s_t=f_t^TM^{-1}f_t$, the update is $\\xi_t^+=\\xi_ts_t/p$ and the returned violation is $\\max_ts_t-p$.\n\n[F3] [[thm-multiplicative-design-update-well-defined-and-terminates]]: each exact update preserves the approximate-design simplex and nonsingularity.\n\n[F4] [[thm-equivalence-violation-bounds-d-optimal-design-gap]]: zero violation certifies D-optimality, and a positive violation bounds every nonsingular competitor's log-determinant gap.\n\n[F5] [[thm-logarithm-derivative-and-integral]] gives $\\log'(x)=1/x>0$ for $x>0$. For $0<a<b$, the logarithm is continuous on $[a,b]$ and differentiable inside, so [[cor-mean-value-theorem]] gives $\\log b-\\log a=(b-a)/c>0$ for some $c\\in(a,b)$. Thus $\\log$ is strictly increasing on $(0,\\infty)$.\n\n## Verification\n\n1.1 For an arbitrary approximate design $\\xi$, write $\\mu=\\sum_t\\xi_tt$ and $\\nu=\\sum_t\\xi_tt^2$. Direct multiplication gives $M(\\xi)=\\begin{bmatrix}1&\\mu\\\\\\mu&\\nu\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\det M(\\xi)=\\nu-\\mu^2$. Since $0\\le t\\le2$, $(t-1)^2\\le1$, so $\\nu-\\mu^2=\\sum_t\\xi_t(t-1)^2-(\\mu-1)^2\\le1$. The equal endpoint design $\\xi^*=(1/2,0,1/2)$ has $M(\\xi^*)=\\begin{bmatrix}1&1\\\\1&2\\end{bmatrix}$ and determinant $1$, so it maximizes the determinant and hence $\\log\\det M$ among nonsingular designs because $\\log$ is strictly increasing by [F5]. [given, F1, F5, algebra]\n\n1.2 For $\\xi^{(0)}$, $M_0=\\begin{bmatrix}1&1\\\\1&5/3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $M_0^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}5/2&-3/2\\\\-3/2&3/2\\end{bmatrix}$. Thus $(s_0,s_1,s_2)=(5/2,1,5/2)$ and $v_0=1/2$. One update gives $\\xi^{(1)}=(5/12,1/6,5/12)$; the update is defined by [F2] and remains nonsingular by [F3]. [given, F2, F3, algebra]\n\n2.1 The updated matrix is $M_1=\\begin{bmatrix}1&1\\\\1&11/6\\end{bmatrix}$, with inverse $M_1^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}11/5&-6/5\\\\-6/5&6/5\\end{bmatrix}$. Therefore $(s_0,s_1,s_2)=(11/5,1,11/5)$ and $v_1=11/5-2=1/5$. With budget $N=1$, the procedure returns the updated design with status budget-exhausted and violation $1/5$. [step 1.2, F2, F3, algebra]\n\n3.1 For the endpoint design $\\xi^*$, $M(\\xi^*)^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2&-1\\\\-1&1\\end{bmatrix}$, so its sensitivities at $0,1,2$ are $2,1,2$ and its violation is zero. By [F4] this is an exact D-optimality certificate; [F4] also bounds the log-determinant gap of the one-update output by $1/5$. The weights are approximate-design weights; integer run counts are a separate problem by [F1]. [step 1.1, step 2.1, F1, F4, algebra] ∎\n",
    "items/lem-log-determinant-concavity-on-positive-definite-matrices.md": "---\nid: lem-log-determinant-concavity-on-positive-definite-matrices\nkind: lemma\ntitle: \"The log determinant is concave on positive-definite matrices\"\nstatus: published\norigin: pipeline\npipeline_run: statistical-models-experiments-62\ndependency_level: 0\ndeps:\n  - prop-standard-coordinate-inner-products\n  - prop-self-adjoint-and-normal-matrix-criteria-in-orthonormal-bases\n  - cor-real-spectral-theorem-for-self-adjoint-endomorphisms\n  - thm-determinant-of-a-triangular-matrix\n  - thm-determinant-multiplicative\n  - def-determinant-of-a-square-matrix\n  - def-matrix-minors-cofactors-and-adjugate\n  - thm-laplace-cofactor-expansion\n  - thm-adjugate-identity-over-a-commutative-ring\n  - prop-matrix-differentials-obey-sum-product-and-adjoint-rules\n  - thm-determinant-differential-and-jacobis-formula\n  - thm-derivative-of-matrix-inversion\n  - prop-differentials-of-trace-and-frobenius-linear-functionals\n  - def-trace-of-a-square-matrix\n  - thm-logarithm-derivative-and-integral\n  - thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives\n  - cor-mean-value-theorem\njustified_by: []\naliases: []\nproof_strategy: direct\nprovenance:\n  statement: literature-derived\n  proof: ai-altered\nverification:\n  precheck: pass\n  judge:\n    model: \"gpt-6-luna\"\n    verdict: pass\n    date: 2026-10-15\nsources:\n  references:\n    - title: \"Stephen Boyd and Lieven Vandenberghe, Convex Optimization, §3.1.5\"\n      url: \"https://web.stanford.edu/~boyd/cvxbook/bv_cvxbook.pdf\"\n---\n\n## Statement\n\nFor $p\\ge1$, if $A_0,A_1\\in\\mathbb R^{p\\times p}$ are symmetric and $v^{\\mathsf T}A_i v>0$ for every nonzero $v\\in\\mathbb R^p$ and $i\\in\\{0,1\\}$, then for every $t\\in[0,1]$,\n$$\\log\\det((1-t)A_0+tA_1)\\ge(1-t)\\log\\det A_0+t\\log\\det A_1.$$\n\n## Facts & Assumptions\n\n**Given:** Two real symmetric positive-definite $p\\times p$ matrices $A_0,A_1$, with $p\\ge1$.\n\n[F1] [[prop-standard-coordinate-inner-products]]: the standard pairing on $\\mathbb R^p$ is $\\langle x,y\\rangle=\\sum_{i<p}x_i y_i$.\n\n[F2] [[prop-self-adjoint-and-normal-matrix-criteria-in-orthonormal-bases]]: $T$ is self-adjoint if and only if $A=A^*$; over $\\mathbb R$, $A^*$ is $A^{\\mathsf T}$.\n\n[F3] [[cor-real-spectral-theorem-for-self-adjoint-endomorphisms]]: a self-adjoint endomorphism of a finite-dimensional real inner-product space has an orthonormal eigenbasis.\n\n[F4] [[thm-determinant-of-a-triangular-matrix]]: the determinant of a triangular matrix is the product of its diagonal entries.\n\n[F5] [[thm-determinant-multiplicative]]: for same-sized square matrices over a commutative ring, $\\det(AB)=\\det(A)\\det(B)$.\n\n[F6] [[def-determinant-of-a-square-matrix]]: $\\det(A):=\\sum_{\\sigma\\in S_n}\\operatorname{sgn}(\\sigma)\\prod_{i<n}a_{\\sigma(i),i}$.\n\n[F7] [[prop-matrix-differentials-obey-sum-product-and-adjoint-rules]]: $d(AB)=dA\\,B+A\\,dB$.\n\n[F8] [[thm-determinant-differential-and-jacobis-formula]]: on the invertible locus, $D\\det(A)[H]=\\det(A)\\operatorname{tr}(A^{-1}H)$.\n\n[F9] [[thm-derivative-of-matrix-inversion]]: for invertible $A$, $D\\operatorname{inv}(A)[H]=-A^{-1}HA^{-1}$.\n\n[F10] [[prop-differentials-of-trace-and-frobenius-linear-functionals]]: $D(\\operatorname{tr})(A)[H]=\\operatorname{tr}(H)$.\n\n[F11] [[def-trace-of-a-square-matrix]]: $\\operatorname{tr}(A)$ is the sum of the diagonal entries of $A$.\n\n[F12] [[def-matrix-minors-cofactors-and-adjugate]]: for a positive-sized square matrix, the cofactors are signed deleted-row-and-column minors and $\\operatorname{adj}(A)=\\operatorname{cof}(A)^{\\mathsf T}$.\n\n[F13] [[thm-laplace-cofactor-expansion]]: for a positive-sized square matrix over a commutative ring, determinant expansion along any row or column is the sum of entries times their corresponding cofactors.\n\n[F14] [[thm-adjugate-identity-over-a-commutative-ring]]: for a positive-sized square matrix over a commutative ring, $A\\operatorname{adj}(A)=\\operatorname{adj}(A)A=\\det(A)I$.\n\n[F15] [[thm-logarithm-derivative-and-integral]]: $\\log'(x)=1/x$ for $x>0$. Under the entrywise identification of a real matrix space with a finite-dimensional Euclidean space, [[thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives]] composes its Fréchet derivatives with the scalar logarithm and an affine matrix path. The path $t\\mapsto A_0+tH$ has derivative $H$ and zero remainder.\n\n[F16] [[cor-mean-value-theorem]]: a real function continuous on a closed interval and differentiable on its interior has an increment equal to the interval length times an interior derivative.\n\n## Proof\n\n**Proof technique:** restrict to a line and compute its second derivative.\n\n1.1 For $t\\in[0,1]$, put $A_t=(1-t)A_0+tA_1$ and $H=A_1-A_0$. For every nonzero $v$, $v^{\\mathsf T}A_tv=(1-t)v^{\\mathsf T}A_0v+t v^{\\mathsf T}A_1v>0$, so $A_t$ is symmetric positive definite. By [F1] the standard coordinate basis is orthonormal, [F2] makes the symmetric matrix self-adjoint, and [F3] gives an orthonormal eigenbasis. Each eigenvalue $\\lambda_i(t)$ is positive because for its unit eigenvector $v_i$, $\\lambda_i(t)=v_i^{\\mathsf T}A_tv_i>0$. [given, F1, F2, F3, algebra]\n\n2.1 In that eigenbasis $A_t=Q\\Lambda Q^{\\mathsf T}$ with $Q Q^{\\mathsf T}=I_p$ and $\\Lambda=\\operatorname{diag}(\\lambda_1(t),\\ldots,\\lambda_p(t))$. By [F4] and [F5], $\\det A_t=\\det Q\\,\\det\\Lambda\\,\\det Q^{\\mathsf T}=\\det\\Lambda=\\prod_i\\lambda_i(t)>0$, since [F5] gives $\\det Q\\det Q^{\\mathsf T}=\\det I_p$ and [F6] gives $\\det I_p=1$ from the Leibniz formula. Thus $g(t)=\\log\\det A_t$ is real-valued on $[0,1]$. [F4, F5, F6, step 1.1, algebra]\n\n3.1 Since $A_t$ is invertible, the determinant differential in [F8] is Jacobi's formula; its cofactor representation and invertible-matrix reduction use [F12], [F13], and [F14]. Because the determinant is differentiable, it is continuous; its positive value at each $A_t$ remains positive on a small neighborhood of that path point, so the logarithm's domain condition in [F15] is met, including at $t=0,1$. The logarithm derivative and total chain rule [F15] then give $g'(t)=(\\det A_t)^{-1}D\\det(A_t)[H]=\\operatorname{tr}(A_t^{-1}H)$. [F8, F12, F13, F14, F15, step 2.1, algebra]\n\n4.1 Differentiate this trace along the same line. The product rule [F7], inverse derivative [F9], and trace derivative [F10], composed with the affine path by [F15], give $g''(t)=-\\operatorname{tr}(A_t^{-1}H A_t^{-1}H)$. [F7, F9, F10, F15, step 3.1, algebra]\n\n5.1 Fix $t$ and define the symmetric matrix $S=A_t^{-1/2}HA_t^{-1/2}$ by diagonalizing $A_t$ from [F3] and taking the positive inverse square roots of its eigenvalues. By [F11], $\\operatorname{tr}(XY)=\\operatorname{tr}(YX)$ because both sides are $\\sum_{i,j}X_{ij}Y_{ji}$ with indices interchanged; applying this identity gives $\\operatorname{tr}(A_t^{-1}HA_t^{-1}H)=\\operatorname{tr}(S^2)=\\sum_{i,j}S_{ij}^2\\ge0$. Hence [step 4.1] implies $g''(t)\\le0$. [F3, F11, step 4.1, algebra]\n\n6.1 The formulas in steps 3.1–4.1 make $g$ differentiable on a neighborhood of $[0,1]$ and $g'$ differentiable there; in particular each is continuous on the closed subintervals used below. For $0\\le r<s\\le1$, apply [F16] to $g'$ on $[r,s]$; step 5.1 gives $g'(s)-g'(r)=g''(c)(s-r)\\le0$ for some $c\\in(r,s)$. Thus $g'$ is nonincreasing. If $0<t<1$, apply [F16] to $g$ on $[0,t]$ and $[t,1]$. The respective slopes equal $g'(a)$ and $g'(b)$ for $a<t<b$, hence $(g(t)-g(0))/t\\ge(g(1)-g(t))/(1-t)$. Rearrangement gives $g(t)\\ge(1-t)g(0)+tg(1)$. At $t=0,1$ it is equality. This is precisely the stated log-determinant chord inequality. [F16, step 3.1, step 4.1, step 5.1, given, algebra] ∎",
    "items/thm-d-optimality-equivalence-theorem.md": "---\nid: thm-d-optimality-equivalence-theorem\nkind: theorem\ntitle: \"Equivalence criterion for D-optimal approximate designs\"\nstatus: published\norigin: pipeline\npipeline_run: statistical-models-experiments-62\ndependency_level: 1\ndeps:\n  - def-approximate-linear-model-design-and-optimality-criteria\n  - lem-log-determinant-concavity-on-positive-definite-matrices\n  - thm-determinant-differential-and-jacobis-formula\n  - def-matrix-minors-cofactors-and-adjugate\n  - thm-laplace-cofactor-expansion\n  - thm-adjugate-identity-over-a-commutative-ring\n  - def-trace-of-a-square-matrix\n  - thm-logarithm-derivative-and-integral\n  - thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives\njustified_by: []\naliases: []\nproof_strategy: direct\nprovenance:\n  statement: literature-derived\n  proof: ai-altered\nverification:\n  precheck: pass\n  judge:\n    model: \"gpt-6-luna\"\n    verdict: pass\n    date: 2026-10-15\nsources:\n  references:\n    - title: \"Stephen Boyd and Lieven Vandenberghe, Convex Optimization, §§3.1.5 and 7.5\"\n      url: \"https://web.stanford.edu/~boyd/cvxbook/bv_cvxbook.pdf\"\n---\n\n## Statement\n\nLet $\\mathcal X$ be a finite nonempty candidate set, $f:\\mathcal X\\to\\mathbb R^p$, and let $\\xi^*$ be a nonsingular approximate design as in [[def-approximate-linear-model-design-and-optimality-criteria]]. Write $M^*=M(\\xi^*)$ and $u^*(x)=f(x)^{\\mathsf T}(M^*)^{-1}f(x)$. Then $\\xi^*$ is D-optimal among nonsingular approximate designs if and only if $u^*(x)\\le p$ for every $x\\in\\mathcal X$. Whenever this holds, $u^*(x)=p$ for every $x\\in\\operatorname{supp}(\\xi^*)$. No existence of a nonsingular optimum is asserted.\n\n## Facts & Assumptions\n\n**Given:** A finite nonempty $\\mathcal X$, $p\\ge1$, a regression-vector map $f$, a nonsingular approximate design $\\xi^*$, and its matrix $M^*$.\n\n[F1] [[def-approximate-linear-model-design-and-optimality-criteria]]: $M(\\xi)=\\sum_{x\\in\\mathcal X}\\xi_x f(x)f(x)^{\\mathsf T}$ for an approximate design $\\xi$.\n\n[F2] [[def-approximate-linear-model-design-and-optimality-criteria]]: D-optimality maximizes $\\log\\det M(\\xi)$ among nonsingular designs.\n\n[F3] [[lem-log-determinant-concavity-on-positive-definite-matrices]]: for positive-definite $A_0,A_1$, $\\log\\det((1-t)A_0+tA_1)\\ge(1-t)\\log\\det A_0+t\\log\\det A_1$ for $0\\le t\\le1$.\n\n[F4] [[thm-determinant-differential-and-jacobis-formula]]: for invertible $A$, $D\\det(A)[H]=\\det(A)\\operatorname{tr}(A^{-1}H)$.\n\n[F5] [[def-trace-of-a-square-matrix]]: $\\operatorname{tr}(A)$ is the sum of the diagonal entries of $A$.\n\n[F6] [[def-matrix-minors-cofactors-and-adjugate]]: for a positive-sized square matrix, the cofactors are signed deleted-row-and-column minors and $\\operatorname{adj}(A)=\\operatorname{cof}(A)^{\\mathsf T}$.\n\n[F7] [[thm-laplace-cofactor-expansion]]: for a positive-sized square matrix over a commutative ring, determinant expansion along any row or column is the sum of entries times their corresponding cofactors.\n\n[F8] [[thm-adjugate-identity-over-a-commutative-ring]]: for a positive-sized square matrix over a commutative ring, $A\\operatorname{adj}(A)=\\operatorname{adj}(A)A=\\det(A)I$.\n\n[F9] [[thm-logarithm-derivative-and-integral]] gives $\\log'(r)=1/r$ for $r>0$; [[thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives]] composes it with the determinant differential and affine matrix paths, using the entrywise Euclidean coordinates on real matrix space. Thus, along an affine path $A_t=A+tH$ with positive determinant, $(\\log\\det A_t)'=\\operatorname{tr}(A_t^{-1}H)$ by [F4]. Determinant continuity keeps its positive value in the logarithm domain locally at every path point.\n\n## Proof\n\n**Proof technique:** use feasible directional derivatives for necessity and concavity for sufficiency.\n\n1.1 Fix $x\\in\\mathcal X$ and let $\\delta_x$ be the design putting weight $1$ at $x$. For $0\\le t<1$, $\\xi_t=(1-t)\\xi^*+t\\delta_x$ has matrix $M_t=(1-t)M^*+t f(x)f(x)^{\\mathsf T}$, which is positive definite because $M^*$ is positive definite and $f(x)f(x)^{\\mathsf T}$ is positive semidefinite. [given, F1, choose, algebra]\n\n1.2 Conversely, suppose $u^*(x)\\le p$ for every candidate. Let $\\eta$ be any nonsingular approximate design and put $N=M(\\eta)$. The segment $M_s=(1-s)M^*+sN$ is positive definite; by [F3], $g(s)=\\log\\det M_s$ is concave. By [F4], with its cofactor and adjugate support [F6], [F7], and [F8], and the logarithm-chain rule [F9], and by [F1], $g'(0)=\\operatorname{tr}((M^*)^{-1}(N-M^*))=\\sum_x\\eta_x u^*(x)-p\\le0$. [given, F1, F3, F4, F6, F7, F8, F9, choose, algebra]\n\n2.1 If $\\xi^*$ is D-optimal, the defining maximum property in [F2] gives $g_x(t)=\\log\\det M_t\\le g_x(0)$ for $0\\le t<1$, so its right derivative at $0$ is nonpositive. By [F4], with its cofactor and adjugate support [F6], [F7], and [F8], the logarithm-chain rule [F9] gives $g_x'(0)=\\operatorname{tr}((M^*)^{-1}(f(x)f(x)^{\\mathsf T}-M^*))=u^*(x)-p$; the trace of the rank-one term is $f(x)^{\\mathsf T}(M^*)^{-1}f(x)$ by the coordinate sum, and $\\operatorname{tr}(I_p)=p$ by [F5]. Thus $u^*(x)\\le p$. Since $x$ was arbitrary, the pointwise bound is necessary. [given, F2, F4, F5, F6, F7, F8, F9, step 1.1, choose, algebra]\n\n2.2 For $0<s<1$, the concavity in step 1.2 gives $g(s)\\ge(1-s)g(0)+sg(1)$, hence $g(1)-g(0)\\le(g(s)-g(0))/s$. Let $s\\downarrow0$; the right derivative already computed in step 1.2 gives $g(1)-g(0)\\le g'(0)\\le0$. Thus $\\log\\det N\\le\\log\\det M^*$. Since $\\eta$ was arbitrary among nonsingular designs, $\\xi^*$ is D-optimal. [F3, step 1.2, algebra]\n\n3.1 Finally, $\\sum_x\\xi_x^*u^*(x)=\\operatorname{tr}((M^*)^{-1}\\sum_x\\xi_x^*f(x)f(x)^{\\mathsf T})=\\operatorname{tr}(I_p)=p$ by [F1] and [F5]. Each term is at most $p$ by the bound assumed in step 1.2, so every term with $\\xi_x^*>0$ equals $p$. This proves equality on the support and completes both directions. [F1, F5, step 1.2, algebra] ∎",
    "items/cor-mean-value-theorem.md": "---\nid: cor-mean-value-theorem\nkind: corollary\ntitle: \"The mean value theorem, as the case $g(x) = x$ of Cauchy's: for $f$ continuous on $[a,b]$ with $a < b$ and differentiable on $(a,b)$ there is $c \\\\in (a,b)$ with $f(b) - f(a) = f'(c)(b-a)$\"\nstatus: published\norigin: session\nprovenance:\n  statement: literature-derived\n  proof: ai-altered\ndeps: [thm-cauchy-mean-value, def-derivative, def-continuity-real, thm-algebra-of-continuous-functions, def-interval, def-function-limit]\njustified_by: []\nforward_refs: [cex-mean-value-theorem-fails-without-continuity-at-the-endpoints]\naliases: [thm-mean-value-theorem, thm-lagrange-mean-value]\nlandmark: true\nshort: \"the mean value theorem\"\nproof_strategy: direct\nverification:\n  repair: research/statistical-models-experiments-62-published-bibliography-cor-mean-value-theorem-repair.json\n  precheck: pass\nsources:\n  scraped: []\n  references:\n    - title: \"Mean value theorem (Wikipedia)\"\n      url: \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mean_value_theorem\"\n    - title: \"W. Rudin, Principles of Mathematical Analysis, 3rd ed., Ch. 5 (Thm 5.10)\"\n    - title: \"J. Lebl, Basic Analysis I, §4.2\"\n      url: \"https://www.jirka.org/ra/\"\n    - title: \"J. Lebl, Basic Analysis I, Mean Value Theorem\"\n      url: \"https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_mvt.html\"\n    - title: \"J. Hunter, An Introduction to Real Analysis\"\n      url: \"https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/intro_analysis_pdf/intro_analysis.pdf\"\npipeline_run: null\n---\n\n## Statement\n\nLet $a, b \\in \\mathbb{R}$ with $a < b$ and let $f : [a,b] \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be\ncontinuous on $[a,b]$ ([[def-continuity-real]], [[def-interval]]) and\ndifferentiable at every point of $(a,b)$ as a function on $[a,b]$\n([[def-derivative]]). Then there is $c \\in (a,b)$ with\n\n$$f(b) - f(a) \\;=\\; f'(c)\\,(b - a) .$$\n\nEquivalently, since $b - a \\ne 0$, there is $c \\in (a,b)$ at which\n$f'(c) = \\bigl(f(b)-f(a)\\bigr)/(b-a)$: the derivative somewhere inside equals\nthe average rate of change across the whole interval.\n\n**Continuity on the closed interval cannot be dropped.** Differentiability at\nevery point of $(a,b)$ alone does not suffice: a function on $[0,1]$,\ndifferentiable at every point of $(0,1)$ with derivative constantly $1$, for\nwhich no $c$ works, is exhibited later on this page as a false statement, and\nthe companion page works the same witness out in full.\n\n## Facts & Assumptions\n\n**Given:** Reals $a < b$ and a function $f : [a,b] \\to \\mathbb{R}$ continuous on $[a,b]$ and differentiable at every point of $(a,b)$.\n\n[L1] Cauchy's mean value theorem ([[thm-cauchy-mean-value]]): for $f, g$ continuous on $[a,b]$ and differentiable at every point of $(a,b)$ there is $c \\in (a,b)$ with $\\bigl(f(b)-f(a)\\bigr)g'(c) = \\bigl(g(b)-g(a)\\bigr)f'(c)$.\n\n[L2] The identity $x \\mapsto x$ is continuous at every point of any $A \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ ([[thm-algebra-of-continuous-functions]], claim 5).\n\n[L3] The identity $g$ on $[a,b]$ is differentiable at every $c \\in [a,b]$ with $g'(c) = 1$: with $a < b$ the set $[a,b]$ is order-convex with at least two elements, so every one of its points is a limit point of it ([[def-derivative]], [[def-interval]]); and the difference quotient of $g$ at $c$ is $(x - c)/(x - c) = 1$ for every $x \\in [a,b]$ with $x \\ne c$, a constant function, whose limit at $c$ is $1$ ([[def-function-limit]], [[def-derivative]]).\n\n## Proof\n\n**Proof technique:** direct.\n\n1.1 Define $g : [a,b] \\to \\mathbb{R}$ by $g(x) := x$. [construct]\n\n2.1 $g$ is continuous on $[a,b]$ by [L2]; it is differentiable at every $c \\in (a,b)$ with $g'(c) = 1$ by [L3]; and $g(b) - g(a) = b - a$. [step 1.1, L2, L3]\n\n3.1 By step 2.1 the pair $f, g$ satisfies every hypothesis of [L1], so there is $c \\in (a,b)$ with $\\bigl(f(b)-f(a)\\bigr)g'(c) = \\bigl(g(b)-g(a)\\bigr)f'(c)$. Substituting $g'(c) = 1$ and $g(b)-g(a) = b-a$ gives $f(b) - f(a) = f'(c)(b-a)$. [step 2.1, L1] ∎\n\n## Remarks\n\n- **The geometric reading, and what it is not.** The conclusion says that some tangent line is parallel to the chord from $(a, f(a))$ to $(b, f(b))$. It does not say which one, it does not say there is only one, and it says nothing at all about $f$ between the endpoints beyond the hypotheses. Every use of the theorem on this page is a use of the equation, never of the picture.\n\n- **Why this is a corollary and not the primitive statement.** [[thm-cauchy-mean-value]] is proved from [[thm-rolle]] with one auxiliary function, and this statement is that theorem at $g(x) = x$; deriving it the other way round, from this statement to Cauchy's, is also possible but needs an auxiliary function of its own, so nothing is saved. What matters is that both rest on Rolle, and Rolle on the extreme value theorem.\n\n- **The two hypotheses are on different sets on purpose.** Continuity is asked for on the closed interval and differentiability only on the open one, so nothing at all is required of the difference quotients at $a$ and at $b$. Weakening the continuity in step with the differentiability, so that both are asked for on $(a,b)$ only, destroys the theorem, which is exactly what [[cex-mean-value-theorem-fails-without-continuity-at-the-endpoints]] shows on the companion page.\n"
  },
